WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:07.417
Secure APIs control what they return as
carefully as what they accept.

2
00:00:07.497 --> 00:00:14.648
A Minimal API endpoint may be close to the
data access code, service call,

3
00:00:14.728 --> 00:00:18.949
or domain object that produced the result.

4
00:00:19.029 --> 00:00:25.829
That closeness can tempt teams to return
internal objects directly.

5
00:00:25.909 --> 00:00:31.771
Response shaping keeps the external
contract deliberate.

6
00:00:31.851 --> 00:00:39.588
Response DTOs help prevent accidental
exposure of internal fields, secrets,

7
00:00:39.668 --> 00:00:46.703
flags, tokens, administrative data, tenant
markers, internal notes,

8
00:00:46.783 --> 00:00:48.658
or workflow state.

9
00:00:48.738 --> 00:00:54.952
The caller should receive the information
needed for the task,

10
00:00:55.032 --> 00:00:59.604
not every property the server happens to
have.

11
00:00:59.684 --> 00:01:08.008
This is especially important for endpoints
that serve multiple client types or user

12
00:01:08.088 --> 00:01:08.791
roles.

13
00:01:08.871 --> 00:01:16.257
Error responses should be useful to
clients but not disclose stack traces,

14
00:01:16.337 --> 00:01:24.544
internal implementation details, secret
names, connection strings, file paths,

15
00:01:24.624 --> 00:01:27.320
or sensitive identifiers.

16
00:01:27.400 --> 00:01:35.724
A consistent error shape helps clients
handle failures while preserving sensitive

17
00:01:35.804 --> 00:01:39.673
diagnostic detail for protected logs.

18
00:01:39.753 --> 00:01:44.912
Status codes should be consistent and
intentional.

19
00:01:44.992 --> 00:01:52.143
A validation failure, authentication
failure, authorization denial,

20
00:01:52.223 --> 00:01:57.381
missing resource, conflict, rate-limit
response,

21
00:01:57.461 --> 00:02:03.323
and unexpected server failure should not
all look the same.

22
00:02:03.403 --> 00:02:08.913
Consistent status behavior helps clients,
operators,

23
00:02:08.993 --> 00:02:13.331
and security reviewers understand the API.

24
00:02:13.411 --> 00:02:21.501
Problem details and typed results can
support predictable API behavior when used

25
00:02:21.581 --> 00:02:22.753
carefully.

26
00:02:22.833 --> 00:02:29.750
They help define response shapes and make
handlers easier to inspect.

27
00:02:29.830 --> 00:02:37.333
The key is discipline: return clear
outcomes, avoid raw exception details,

28
00:02:37.413 --> 00:02:43.041
and keep sensitive data out of
client-facing responses.

29
00:02:43.121 --> 00:02:50.858
Logging can preserve operational detail
that should not be sent to the client.

30
00:02:50.938 --> 00:02:58.090
Safe logs can include correlation IDs,
route names, status categories,

31
00:02:58.170 --> 00:03:06.376
validation categories, and failure context
without copying secrets or sensitive

32
00:03:06.456 --> 00:03:09.504
request and response content.

33
00:03:09.584 --> 00:03:18.260
The client response and operator
diagnostic record should serve different audiences.
